                <pb facs="0235" n="139" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg139"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 2—Translation]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Envoy Extraordinary at Damascus</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Alype</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of the French High
                        Commissioner to the States of Syria and the Lebanon</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">De Jouvenel</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Dean of
                        the Consular Corps at Damascus</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Smart</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Damascus</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-02-19"><hi rendition="#italic">February 19,
                        1926</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2924/C</seg></opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dean</hi>: The 9th of February last, you were
                    good enough to call my attention to the anxiety of the Consular Corps on the
                    present situation in Damascus.</p>
                <p>I hastened to bring this matter to the attention of General Andréa, and I have
                    the honor to transmit to you herewith a copy of his answer.</p>
                <p>I add that the fate of the foreign colonies is the object of all the solicitude
                    of the Mandatory Power in Syria, and I beg you to accept, Mr. Dean, the
                    assurance of my high consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pierre Alype</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
